# Copy to .env. One value is required — AGENT_NAME — two more before the agent
# can reach GitHub, and AGENT_ARCHIVE_REPO before anything can archive a
# transcript. Everything else below is commented out and shows the value it
# derives to: uncomment a line only to depart from it.
#
# The chain runs downwards, and an override becomes the input to every step
# under it. Setting AGENT_USER moves the home, the checkout, the volume, the
# project, the image tag and the vault projects together, rather than leaving
# five of them naming the old agent.
#
# Every variable, by category, is in VARIABLES.md; the records behind the
# shapes here are in docs/configuration.md.
#
# Three things are deliberately NOT here, because they are lists and sentences
# rather than values: the vault entries that are not credentials, this agent's
# own secret shapes, and the community it belongs to. They live in three
# untracked files under image/, which `just setup` makes from their committed
# examples. see docs/configuration.md#the-three-files-that-are-yours

# What the agent is called. Everything else is built from this.
AGENT_NAME=Agent

# The human the agent reports to, named in the messages it reads and in the
# marker that says a message came from a person rather than from the schedule.
# Defaults to `Operator`. Changing it later breaks `chat --continue` for
# earlier conversations: that marker is matched literally in transcripts
# already written.
# see docs/configuration.md#the-operator-marker-is-matched-literally
#OPERATOR_NAME=

# The same person's GitHub account. It renders the autoMode block in
# image/managed-settings.json, where `<handle>/*` appears as a trusted owner;
# without it nothing connects that owner to the person the agent writes about
# by name. Empty is a real answer — the classifier is then told nothing rather
# than told someone else's.
#OPERATOR_GITHUB_HANDLE=

# The agent's own repository, over SSH, and the address it commits as. Use the
# account's own noreply address, copied from its GitHub email settings: the
# numeric id is not derivable from the handle, which is why this one is asked
# for and the name beside it is not.
#
# `<NAME>` is AGENT_NAME uppercased, dashes as underscores — an agent called
# Demo reads `DEMO_REPO`. Uncomment these two under that name.
#
# They are required and nonetheless ship commented out, because `<NAME>_REPO=`
# is not a name a shell can hold and `just` refuses the whole file on one such
# line.
# see docs/configuration.md#the-placeholder-lines-ship-commented-out
#<NAME>_REPO=git@github.com:youraccount/youragent.git
#<NAME>_GIT_EMAIL=00000000+agent@users.noreply.github.com

# The agent's own domain, if it has one. It renders the autoMode block too, so
# the classifier knows which zone is the agent's to publish on — a hostname it
# cannot place is judged as a stranger's. Empty means it has none, which is
# true of a fresh installation.
#<NAME>_DOMAIN=

# THE MODEL A SESSION RUNS ON — an alias such as `opus` or `sonnet`, rendered
# into managed settings at build so it holds for every kind of session and
# the agent cannot change it. `sonnet` unless set; set `opus` or `opus[1m]` before the first
# build if that is more than you mean to spend.
#<NAME>_MODEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived. Each line shows what it becomes when left alone.
#
#   <NAME>_GIT_NAME   = AGENT_NAME
#   AGENT_USER       = AGENT_NAME, lowercased, spaces closed up
#   AGENT_HOME       = /home/$AGENT_USER
#   <NAME>_REPO_DIR   = $AGENT_HOME/<the repository's own name>
#   AGENT_VOLUME     = $AGENT_USER-home
#   RUNNER_PROJECT    = $AGENT_USER-runner        (Compose's project name)
#
# The vault's three Bitwarden projects, and the mirror workflow's file name in
# the archive:
#
#   BWS_VAULT_PROJECT           = $AGENT_USER-acquired
#   BWS_VAULT_READABLE_PROJECT  = $AGENT_USER-provisioned
#   BWS_VAULT_TEST_PROJECT      = $AGENT_USER-test
#   AGENT_ARCHIVE_WORKFLOW        = mirror-$AGENT_USER.yml
#
# Changing AGENT_USER, AGENT_HOME or <NAME>_REPO_DIR on an installation that
# has already run is a migration, not an edit: the volume holds absolute paths,
# and none of them move on their own.
# see docs/configuration.md#changing-the-identity-later-is-a-migration
#
#<NAME>_GIT_NAME=
#AGENT_USER=
#AGENT_HOME=
#<NAME>_REPO_DIR=
#AGENT_VOLUME=
#RUNNER_PROJECT=
#BWS_VAULT_PROJECT=
#BWS_VAULT_READABLE_PROJECT=
#BWS_VAULT_TEST_PROJECT=
#AGENT_ARCHIVE_WORKFLOW=
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Who owns the transcript archive — the one thing that cannot be derived, since
# it is usually not the agent's own account. Needed by `just collect`, `just
# read`, `just sessions`, `just mirror-status` and `just setup-archive`; leave
# it unset and those say so rather than guessing.
#AGENT_ARCHIVE_REPO=youraccount/youragent-archive

# Where that clone lives. Unset means `archive/` inside this checkout,
# gitignored like `deployed/` and made by `just setup-archive`, so a clone of
# this repository arranges nothing outside its own directory. Set it to keep
# the archive somewhere else — a sibling checkout, say. A relative value
# counts from this checkout, wherever `just` was run from.
#AGENT_ARCHIVE=archive

# The checkout cron runs from. Unset means `deployed/` inside this checkout,
# gitignored, a worktree `just deploy` resets to what you are shipping and
# `just schedule` writes the cron line for — so what runs unattended is what
# was deployed, whatever this checkout looks like meanwhile. Set it to keep
# that worktree somewhere else. A relative value counts from this checkout,
# wherever `just` was run from.
#RUNNER_DEPLOYED=deployed

# Where the drift audit keeps its world: its clone of the archive's mirror of
# the agent's memory, the two anchors, the reports and the log. Unset means
# `monitor/` inside this checkout, gitignored and made by `just drift-audit`
# on its first run. Set it to keep that state somewhere else, or to point one
# run at a scratch directory. A relative value counts from this checkout,
# wherever `just` was run from.
#RUNNER_MONITOR=monitor

# How long transcripts survive in the volume, in days, baked into managed
# settings as `cleanupPeriodDays`. Unset is Claude Code's own default of 30.
#
# It is not what keeps the record — `just collect` puts every session's
# transcript in the archive minutes after the session ends — it is how long the
# agent can still read and re-measure its own past from inside the container.
# Raising it costs disk in the volume and, on every collection, a copy of the
# whole surviving corpus out of it.
#
# A whole number of days, 1 or more; the build refuses anything else, `0`
# included — it is valid JSON and means delete everything at the next session
# start.
#AGENT_TRANSCRIPT_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Testing only: any non-empty value skips the clone, so the image can be
# exercised without a repository or a key GitHub knows about. It announces
# itself on every start; leave it unset for a real session.
#AGENT_SKIP_CLONE=1

# Whether this host refuses a session that is over budget. Empty or false is
# off, and off means nothing here decides: a session starts whatever the
# account has spent, and reads its own usage only so the agent can see where it
# stands.
#
# It lives on the host because reading usage needs a login carrying the
# `user:profile` scope, which only the interactive `claude login` grants.
# see docs/budget.md#userprofile-and-why-the-guard-runs-on-the-host
#
# Turn it on only while one account is behind both: the endpoint reports the
# account, not the credential, so what this host reads is what the session will
# spend only while they are the same.
#
# Only exactly `true` arms it — not `yes`, not `1`, not `TRUE`; anything else
# is off. `just verify` prints what it is set to and what a session is
# therefore told, so an unrecognised value is loud rather than silently off.
# see docs/budget.md#exactly-true-arms-the-guard
#
# The four percentages below are read on the host too, and by compose.
# `just run --ignore-budget` overrides this per run, whichever way it is set.
ACCOUNT_BUDGET_GUARD=

# The budget gate. The agent starts a session only while the account sits under
# an allowance that climbs from START at the window's reset to CAP just
# before the next one — so a weekly cap of 60 does not mean 60% may be spent
# on Monday. START is what keeps the first minute of a window from refusing
# everything: at a reset a pure ramp allows nothing at all.
#
# Percentages of the whole account, not of the agent's own share, deliberately:
# what protects your own work is a line the total stays under, whoever spent
# it. Unset is not "no limit" — the gate refuses to answer and no session
# starts.
ACCOUNT_BUDGET_SESSION_START=20
ACCOUNT_BUDGET_SESSION_CAP=50
ACCOUNT_BUDGET_WEEKLY_START=10
ACCOUNT_BUDGET_WEEKLY_CAP=60

# How long a usage reading may be reused, in minutes, on the host and in the
# container alike. The endpoint allows five requests per five minutes, counted
# against the account rather than the credential, and a minutely `just run`
# spends that allowance on its own — leaving none for the session's own read,
# the status snapshot, or any Claude Code you have open.
#
# Only the utilisation goes stale: the allowance is still recomputed from the
# current clock, and utilisation moves only while a session runs, which is when
# the lock is refusing anyway.
#
# Empty or unset is five, not a refusal — the opposite of the four
# ACCOUNT_BUDGET_ settings above, whose prefix this shares: those guard your
# week and must refuse what they cannot read, while this only decides how often
# the endpoint is asked. `0` turns the cache off; a word, a negative, or
# anything above 60 is refused, because `300` is what seconds typed where
# minutes were meant looks like.
# see docs/budget.md#the-endpoint-allows-five-requests-per-five-minutes
#ACCOUNT_BUDGET_CACHE_MINUTES=5

# How long after the archive's mirror last ran a session may ask it to run
# again — in minutes, read on the host only. The container is never told about
# it and could do nothing with it.
#
# The archive mirrors the memory hourly on a GitHub schedule, and GitHub runs
# schedules best-effort; the mirror can only preserve a rewrite back to its
# last run, so a dropped hour is history that was never seen. `just run` and
# `just chat` therefore ask for a mirror at the end of every session, which is
# the moment the memory actually moved. The number is counted from whatever ran
# last — the schedule's own run included — so it holds the total at about one
# mirror an hour rather than one an hour plus one a session.
#
# Empty, unset, or not a number means every session asks. That is the opposite
# of ACCOUNT_BUDGET_GUARD above, on purpose: that one guards the operator's
# week and an unreadable threshold must refuse, while this is a cost knob, the
# direction with no undo is a mirror that did not run, and an extra run costs a
# minute.
# see docs/archive.md#asking-the-mirror-to-run
#AGENT_ARCHIVE_MIRROR_COOLDOWN=

# How long an unattended session may run before the host says so on the Windows
# desktop — in minutes, read on the host only. The container is never told
# about it and could do nothing with it.
#
# A session that hangs is the one failure nothing else reports: it never
# reaches the end of `just run`, so there is no exit status to notice, and
# every wake-up afterwards finds the lock held and stands down at 75, which is
# exactly what a healthy cooldown looks like from the outside. 120 is more than
# twice the longest session that has ever finished.
#
# Conversations are never counted — `just chat` legitimately runs for hours,
# and you are sitting in it. An explicit 0 turns it off, which is a decision;
# anything else that is not a number falls back to 120 rather than to off,
# because a typo must not silently disable the only report a wedge ever
# produces.
# see docs/schedule.md#the-wedge-alarm
#RUNNER_WEDGE_MINUTES=120

# The vault — Bitwarden Secrets Manager, one access token for a machine account
# scoped to two projects: read on the provisioned one, read and write on the
# acquired one. That split is what lets the agent store a secret it acquired
# without being able to overwrite one you gave it. Their names derive from
# AGENT_USER, so create them under that name and there is nothing to configure
# here.
#
# The point of it is that a new secret costs you a paste into Bitwarden and
# nothing here: no new variable, no rebuild, no restore. Unset is not a failure
# — the vault is simply not configured, and `vault` in the container says so.
# Bitwarden shows an access token once and never again; a lost one is
# re-issued, not recovered.
# see docs/vault.md
BWS_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Which Bitwarden server issued it. `bws` defaults to the US one, and a
# token from bitwarden.eu is rejected there as `invalid_client` — a message
# that reads as a bad token and is not. Leave empty for US.
#   EU:  https://vault.bitwarden.eu
#BWS_SERVER_URL=
